s assess the approved formal debate, not the complete event."},{"id":"craig-dacey-god-existence-2004","number":"273","notice":"This assessment covers both opening statements, both rounds of rebuttal, and both closing statements (approximately 5:00–1:40:07). The entire audience Q&A, including both speakers’ answers and dependent replies, is excluded. Neither side receives credit or penalty for excluded material; these scores assess the approved formal debate, not the complete event."},{"id":"shermer-scrivener-good-without-god-2024","number":"277","notice":"By explicit editorial approval, this assessment scores only Michael Shermer and Glen Scrivener across the complete substantive exchange. Moderator Vince Vitale supplies substantive philosophical questions and challenges beyond the ordinary incidental-host allowance; his contributions remain context, and the debaters’ own replies remain eligible for assessment. No argument or score is assigned to a debater merely because Vitale advances it. Unequal questioning is considered when selecting moves and evaluating opportunities to respond. This is a recording-specific exception, not an assessment of a strictly two-speaker recording."},{"id":"lennox-tooley-atheism-christianity-2013","number":"278","notice":"The available recording ends after Michael Tooley’s closing remarks. A contemporary account also describes a closing statement by John Lennox, which is absent from the versions located. This assessment covers the available recording; neither speaker is penalized for material or replies missing from it."},{"id":"slick-barker-god-existence-2014","number":"279","notice":"This assessment uses the recoverable portions of the recording. A 33-second passage at the start of Matt Slick’s rebuttal (41:07.52–41:40.64) could not be reliably transcribed and is excluded. Neither speaker receives credit or a penalty for that passage, and no claim that an argument went unanswered relies on the missing material."},{"id":"turek-normark-